From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Sun, 19 Apr 2026 15:24:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] board: mono: gateway-dk: add per-component self-tests

Adds the per-component self-test modules invoked from misc_init_r() via
the harness in the previous patch. Each one probes its target and
reports PASS/FAIL with a small detail string:

  - clock_gen.c        - 6V49205B clock generator (Sys_CCB output)
  - ddr_sanity.c       - bit-pattern probe of DDR4 banks 0 and 1
  - fan_controller.c   - EMC2302 fan controller, tach + RPM
  - led_controller.c   - LP5810A status LED (also surfaces the
                         aggregate self-test verdict on the front
                         panel via lp5810a_set_status())
  - retimer.c          - DS100DF410 SerDes retimer device ID
  - rtc.c              - PCF2131 real-time clock
  - security_fuses.c   - SFP/SNVS fuse state: OTPMK, SRK hash, ITS,
                         write protect and the SecMon state; a damaged
                         OTPMK or DRV is a warning, not a failure
  - sfp_i2c_mux.c      - resets the SFP-side PCA9545 mux into a
                         known-good state, pulsing RESET# on
                         GPIO3_24 when a module holding SDA low has
                         wedged i2c1
  - temp_sensors.c     - TMP431 CPU and board temperature
  - typec_controller.c - HD3SS3220/TUSB322 USB Type-C controller
  - voltage_sensors.c  - eight INA234 rails behind a PCA9545

The SFP mux recovers over RESET# rather than its control register,
because the failure it has to clear takes that register with it. A
module caught mid-transfer by a reset keeps driving SDA low, and the
cages stay powered across a warm reset so it never reinitialises; it
holds the line through a channel that is still selected, so nothing on
i2c1 answers - the mux control address included - until RESET# is
pulsed. A bus recovered that way still passes, reported as "Reset OK
(recovered wedged bus)" so the event stays visible.

The SecMon state on the fuse line says whether the secure world's keys
are device-unique on this boot: the SEC uses the OTPMK only in the
trusted and secure states, which only a ROM-validated boot reaches, so
"OTPMK set, SecMon non-secure" is a burned key that nothing uses yet.

cmd_otpmk.c adds the otpmk command. 'otpmk gen' draws 256 bits from the
CAAM RNG, Hamming encodes them and loads the fuse mirror registers
without the key ever being printed; 'otpmk commit' programs the mirrors
into the fuses. The LS1046A data sheet (Table 6 note 2) allows nothing
but programming while TA_PROG_SFP is above GND, fuse block reads
included, so every check runs in gen, with the pin at GND. gen refuses
when the OTPMK is already programmed, the block is write-protected or
reports a Hamming error, the mirrors set ITS with a blank SRK hash, or
the RNG returns a key with a blank or repeated half. After loading it
confirms the key through SNVS_HPSR, requires SVHESR clear and every
other mirror word unchanged, and lists the non-zero words PROGFB will
burn with the key. commit reads nothing: it refuses only when gen has
not loaded a key in this session, and leaves the verdict to the next
boot's self-test. gen is refused once a key is loaded or a PROGFB has
been issued, so it never reads the fuse block after the operator has
been told to raise the pin. The SFP and SNVS register definitions both
files use live in security_fuses.h.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [product-specific]

Signed-off-by: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
